 this plot where the actual  name of the state is written out  and then in red we see  superimposed on this plot the directions  of the of the loadings  for the principal components himself the  the direction vectors  and so here we see that the  this first axis is largely due to the  the three types of crime murder assault  and rape  right  and so here's the one direction  so remember the component was negative  it had negative loadings  and so negative scores mean negative  times negative is positive so these are  the states in this end that have got  overall high crime so we see florida  over there nevada california  michigan new mexico  and in the other end of the scale are  those other states with with low total  crime so maine  north dakota new hampshire  um so this first axis is really to do  with the amount of crime in the state  and then the second axis we saw was  mostly loaded with  the loading was mostly urban population  and so this access is really about  whether the state is is has got a large  urban population or not  and  that also had a negative  loading so  new jersey has got a high urban  population  arkansas is on the lower side  mississippi north carolina much lower  okay so one plot and you get to see  you get to a nice summary of the  principal components so this is called a  biplot the word by mean in both the the  loadings and the and the the principal  component scores are put in one plot  okay  so principal components very popular i  encourage you to to to learn more about  them use them  and and  incorporate them in your data analysis
 

